## Proposed additive API

Add an optional prop to [DocumentPicker.tsx](<case>/input/DocumentPicker.tsx):

```tsx
type Props = {
  documents: Document[];
  onSelect: (id: string) => void;
  eligibleIds?: ReadonlySet<string>;
};
```

- Omitted `eligibleIds`: every document remains selectable.
- An empty set: every document is disabled.
- A supplied set: only documents whose IDs are included are selectable. Unknown IDs have no effect.

Existing consumers require no changes:

```tsx
<DocumentPicker documents={documents} onSelect={onSelect} />

<DocumentPicker
  documents={documents}
  onSelect={onReviewSelect}
  eligibleIds={reviewEligibleIds}
/>
```

## Decision ownership

**The parent owns eligibility policy.** It supplies the eligible IDs; the picker applies them without knowing review-specific rules. Replace the set when eligibility changes rather than mutating it in place.

**Each picker owns its search input.** Keep the existing component-local `useState('')`. Two separately rendered instances already have independent queries. Do not lift search state or add an Effect to synchronize it.

Keep the current filtering and sorting unchanged: case-insensitive title substring matching, followed by `title.localeCompare`. Eligibility must not filter the displayed list.

Within `visible.map`, derive selection availability directly from the current prop:

```tsx
const selectable = eligibleIds === undefined || eligibleIds.has(document.id);

return (
  <li key={document.id}>
    <button
      type="button"
      disabled={!selectable}
      onClick={() => {
        if (selectable) onSelect(document.id);
      }}
    >
      {document.title}
    </button>
  </li>
);
```

Native `disabled` supplies disabled semantics and prevents ordinary mouse and keyboard activation. No additional state, Effect, or memoization is needed. This design uses APIs compatible with React 18.3.1.

## Proposed checks

| Consumer/scenario | Expected result |
| --- | --- |
| Existing consumer omits the prop | All matching documents remain selectable; callback receives the selected ID. |
| Review picker supplies a subset | All search matches remain visible; ineligible buttons are disabled and cannot invoke selection. |
| Empty set / unknown IDs | Empty set disables all; unknown IDs enable nothing extra. |
| Parent changes eligibility | Buttons reflect the new set without resetting search. |
| Either picker searches | Existing matching and ordering remain unchanged; the other instance’s input and results stay independent. |
| Both pickers are rendered | Selecting in either invokes that instance’s callback. |

