## Proposed design

Add an optional `eligibleIds` prop to [DocumentPicker.tsx](<case>/input/DocumentPicker.tsx:4):

```tsx
type Props = {
  documents: Document[];
  onSelect: (id: string) => void;
  eligibleIds?: ReadonlySet<string>;
};
```

Contract:

- Omitted: every document is selectable, preserving existing consumers.
- Provided: only IDs in the set are selectable.
- Empty set: every document remains visible when it matches search, but selection is disabled.
- IDs absent from `documents` have no effect.

Inside `DocumentPicker`, accept the new prop and derive eligibility during rendering:

```tsx
<ul>
  {visible.map(document => {
    const canSelect = eligibleIds === undefined ||
      eligibleIds.has(document.id);

    return (
      <li key={document.id}>
        <button
          type="button"
          disabled={!canSelect}
          onClick={() => {
            if (canSelect) onSelect(document.id);
          }}
        >
          {document.title}
        </button>
      </li>
    );
  })}
</ul>
```

Native `disabled` provides disabled interaction semantics. The handler also explicitly enforces the selection condition.

### Ownership and behavior

**The review parent owns eligibility policy.** It supplies the current eligible IDs; the picker applies membership without embedding review-specific rules. Derive membership from current props rather than copying it into state or synchronizing it through an Effect. When eligibility changes, the parent should supply a new set through a rendering update.

**Each picker owns its search input.** Retain the existing `useState('')` inside `DocumentPicker`. Two mounted instances therefore have independent queries. Keep component identity stable; changing eligibility should not change the picker’s key or reset its input.

**The shared picker owns search and ordering.** Preserve the existing case-insensitive title substring filter and `localeCompare` sort exactly. Do not filter by eligibility. The current `.filter(...).sort(...)` sorts a new array, so it does not reorder the supplied `documents` array.

Example consumers:

```tsx
<DocumentPicker
  documents={documents}
  onSelect={onSelectDocument}
/>

<DocumentPicker
  documents={documents}
  onSelect={onSelectReviewDocument}
  eligibleIds={reviewEligibleIds}
/>
```

This uses APIs compatible with React 18.3.1 and requires no new hooks, Effects, or memoization.

## Planned checks

| Consumer / scenario | Expected result |
| --- | --- |
| Existing consumer omits `eligibleIds` | All matching documents remain selectable; callback receives the clicked ID. |
| Review consumer supplies a subset | All search matches remain in the same order; only eligible matches can trigger selection. |
| Empty set or unknown IDs | No unintended selectable documents or extra rows. |
| Eligibility changes while searching | Buttons update without clearing the query. |
| Both instances open | Typing or clearing either query leaves the other unchanged. |
| Search and interaction regression | Preserve case-insensitive matching, title ordering, Korean IME input, labels, enabled-button keyboard activation, and disabled-button behavior. |

